# mef firewall rules v1 (example)
# NOTE: mef firewall fmt will expand missing fields to canonical form.
# NOTE: iif=any / oif=any do NOT match loopback (lo). Loopback is explicit.

# ---- defaults ----
policy dir=in  action=drop
policy dir=fwd action=drop
policy dir=out action=accept  # permissive by default (tighten to drop later, see RESTRICTIVE OUTBOUND below)

# ---- always allow loopback ----
rule dir=in  action=accept proto=all iif=lo  comment="loopback in"
rule dir=out action=accept proto=all oif=lo  comment="loopback out"

# ---- allow established/related (non-loopback) ----
rule dir=in  action=accept proto=all ct=established,related comment="ct established/related in"
rule dir=out action=accept proto=all ct=established,related comment="ct established/related out"

# ---- inbound services (WAN auto) ----

# FTP example (uncomment if running FTP server):
# rule dir=in action=accept proto=tcp iif=auto dport=21 comment="FTP"

# SSH: OPEN by default for initial setup - RESTRICT THIS AFTER TESTING!
# After confirming access, uncomment src= lines below and remove the open rule
rule dir=in action=accept proto=tcp iif=auto dport=22 comment="SSH - OPEN (RESTRICT THIS!)"

# SSH restricted examples (use these INSTEAD of open rule above):
# rule dir=in action=accept proto=tcp iif=auto src=YOUR.IP.ADDR.HERE dport=22 comment="SSH admin IP"
# rule dir=in action=accept proto=tcp iif=auto src=185.185.168.0/22 dport=22 comment="SSH admin network"

# HTTP / HTTPS examples (uncomment if running web server):
# rule dir=in action=accept proto=tcp iif=auto dport=80  comment="HTTP"
# rule dir=in action=accept proto=tcp iif=auto dport=443 comment="HTTPS"

# SMTP / Submission examples (uncomment if running mail server):
# rule dir=in action=accept proto=tcp iif=auto dport=25  comment="SMTP"
# rule dir=in action=accept proto=tcp iif=auto dport=587 comment="SUBMISSION"

# IMAP / POP3 examples (uncomment if running mail server):
# rule dir=in action=accept proto=tcp iif=auto dport=143 comment="IMAP"
# rule dir=in action=accept proto=tcp iif=auto dport=993 comment="IMAPS"
# rule dir=in action=accept proto=tcp iif=auto dport=110 comment="POP3"
# rule dir=in action=accept proto=tcp iif=auto dport=995 comment="POP3S"

# ICMP (optional, for troubleshooting)
rule dir=in action=accept proto=icmp  iif=auto comment="ICMPv4 ping"
rule dir=in action=accept proto=icmp6 iif=auto comment="ICMPv6 ping"

# ---- outbound (WAN auto) ----
rule dir=out action=accept proto=all oif=auto comment="OUTBOUND"

# ============================================================================
# RECOMMENDATION: RESTRICTIVE OUTBOUND FILTERING
# ============================================================================
#
# Currently, outbound is PERMISSIVE (accept all).
# This is safe for initial setup, but security best-practice is to restrict
# outbound to only services you actually need.
#
# TO ENABLE RESTRICTIVE MODE:
#   1. Replace "policy dir=out action=accept" at top with:
#      policy dir=out action=drop
#   2. Uncomment the rules below (or copy/adapt them for your services)
#   3. Test thoroughly (especially SSH, DNS, updates)
#   4. Remember: stateful firewall auto-allows responses via ct=established,related
#
# HOW IT WORKS (stateful firewall):
#   - When a server process initiates outbound connection (e.g., HTTP client),
#     firewall tracks the flow and automatically allows responses back.
#   - When a client INITIATES inbound connection (e.g., client connects to port 80),
#     firewall allows incoming, and the server's response (ct=established,related)
#     is auto-allowed outbound regardless of port restrictions.
#
#   Example: Server runs HTTP (port 80), client initiates:
#   1. Client → Server:80 (allowed by inbound rule)
#   2. Server:random_port → Client (allowed by ct=established,related rule)
#
# ---- BEGIN RESTRICTIVE RULES (uncomment and adapt) ----
#
# # Outbound: established connections (responses to inbound requests)
# rule dir=out action=accept proto=all oif=auto ct=established,related comment="ct established/related out"
#
# # Outbound: HTTP/HTTPS (for outbound client requests, e.g., curl, wget, package managers)
# rule dir=out action=accept proto=tcp oif=auto dport=80  comment="HTTP out"
# rule dir=out action=accept proto=tcp oif=auto dport=443 comment="HTTPS out"
#
# # Outbound: DNS (required for hostname resolution)
# rule dir=out action=accept proto=tcp oif=auto dport=53 comment="DNS TCP out"
# rule dir=out action=accept proto=udp oif=auto dport=53 comment="DNS UDP out"
#
# # Outbound: DHCP (if using DHCP client for IP assignment)
# rule dir=out action=accept proto=udp oif=auto dport=67:68 comment="DHCP out"
#
# # Outbound: ICMP (for ping, traceroute, diagnostics)
# rule dir=out action=accept proto=icmp  oif=auto comment="ICMPv4 out"
# rule dir=out action=accept proto=icmp6 oif=auto comment="ICMPv6 out"
#
# ---- END RESTRICTIVE RULES ----
#
# ADD MORE AS NEEDED:
#   - SMTP out (port 25): if server sends mail
#   - IMAP/POP3 out (port 143/110): if connecting to external mail servers
#   - NTP out (port 123 UDP): if time sync needed
#   - Custom app ports: if application needs outbound to specific services
#
# ONCE RESTRICTIVE RULES ARE IN PLACE:
#   - Default policy is drop, all other outbound is blocked
#   - Add rules as needed for new services

